selftext:
Hey folks! I am making a Java caravan shmup in my free time called Rebel Sector. The playtest is on itch!

[https://moselysoftware.itch.io/rebel-sector](https://moselysoftware.itch.io/rebel-sector)

So what is a ConcurrentModificationException?

**A ConcurrentModificationException can happen when a Java collection is changed while it is being iterated over.**

For example:

java

    for (Entity enemy : enemies) {
        if (enemy.health
     <= 0) {
            enemies.remove(enemy);
        }
    }

Removing an entity inside the loop can cause an error or cause other entities to be skipped.

# My solution: a recycle bin

Instead of removing an entity immediately, I queue it for removal:

java

    if (enemy.isDyingFinished()) {
        game.queueEntityRemoval(enemy);
    }

The entity stays in its current list until the frame is complete. Then I flush the recycle bin:

java

    public void flushRecycleBin() {
        List<Entity> entitiesToFree =
            new ArrayList<>(recycleBin);

        recycleBin.clear();

        for (Entity entity : entitiesToFree) {
            freeEntity(entity);
        }
    }



# Enemy system

The enemy system handles movement, collisions, damage, and enemies leaving the play area.

When an enemy is defeated, it either starts its death animation or is queued immediately:

java

    if (Main.isObstacle(defeatedType)) {
        game.queueEntityRemoval(enemy);
    } else {
        enemy.startDying();
    }




# Rendering system

The rendering system checks the recycle bin before drawing entities:

java

    private static boolean isQueuedForRemoval(
        Entity entity,
        List<Entity> recycleBin
    ) {
        return entity != null
            && recycleBin.contains(entity);
    }

Queued entities are skipped by the renderer, so they do not appear for an extra frame.

It also works well with object pooling because an entity is not returned to its pool until the frame is finished.

How do you handle entity removal in your game?
